WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.880
Two beakers, each with twenty five point zero

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:06.000
zero millilitres of acid at zero point one

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:08.320
zero zero moles per litre.

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:11.920
One is hydrochloric acid, one is ethanoic acid.

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:15.120
Add the same sodium hydroxide to each, measure

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:18.000
the pH after every portion, and plot it.

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:20.640
You get two very different curves.

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:23.760
Every bend in them carries information: whether the

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:26.080
acid is strong or weak, how much there

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:27.840
is, even its K a.

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:30.000
So how do you read one?

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:33.600
Each curve plots the pH in the flask

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:36.640
against the volume of sodium hydroxide added.

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:39.520
Start at the left edge, before any base.

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:43.120
Hydrochloric acid starts at pH one point zero

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:45.920
zero zero, because it's fully ionised.

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:49.520
Ethanoic acid starts at two point eight seven,

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:51.840
because a weak acid stays mostly as whole

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:54.640
molecules: only about one in a hundred has

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:55.360
ionised.

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:58.960
At the same concentration, a higher starting pH

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:00.640
points to a weaker acid.

00:01:01.867 --> 00:01:04.106
Now the first twenty millilitres.

00:01:04.347 --> 00:01:07.706
The hydrochloric acid curve stays low, rising only

00:01:07.867 --> 00:01:10.986
slowly, because plenty of hydronium is still left

00:01:11.027 --> 00:01:11.866
to react.

00:01:12.107 --> 00:01:15.146
The ethanoic acid curve climbs quickly, then levels

00:01:15.227 --> 00:01:15.786
off.

00:01:16.027 --> 00:01:18.666
Each portion of base turns some ethanoic acid

00:01:18.747 --> 00:01:21.386
into ethanoate, and a weak acid with its

00:01:21.467 --> 00:01:23.386
conjugate base is a buffer.

00:01:23.627 --> 00:01:26.346
That gentle stretch is the buffer region, and

00:01:26.427 --> 00:01:28.026
it comes before the jump.

00:01:29.234 --> 00:01:32.033
The moles say each flask reaches equivalence at

00:01:32.114 --> 00:01:34.033
twenty five millilitres.

00:01:34.154 --> 00:01:37.873
So halfway, at twelve point five zero millilitres,

00:01:37.954 --> 00:01:40.873
half the ethanoic acid has become ethanoate.

00:01:41.154 --> 00:01:43.793
Equal amounts of the two partners, and as

00:01:43.834 --> 00:01:46.313
in the buffer video, the pH equals the

00:01:46.354 --> 00:01:47.153
p K a.

00:01:47.634 --> 00:01:49.873
Read it off the curve: four point seven

00:01:50.034 --> 00:01:50.913
four.

00:01:51.034 --> 00:01:52.993
So K a is ten to the minus

00:01:53.154 --> 00:01:55.793
four point seven four, one point eight times

00:01:55.874 --> 00:01:57.473
ten to the minus five.

00:01:57.554 --> 00:01:59.873
That's the acid's own constant, read from a

00:01:59.954 --> 00:02:00.553
graph.

00:02:00.754 --> 00:02:02.913
A strong acid curve has no such point

00:02:02.994 --> 00:02:03.553
to read.

00:02:04.767 --> 00:02:07.166
On the curve, the equivalence point sits in

00:02:07.247 --> 00:02:09.086
the middle of the steepest part.

00:02:09.567 --> 00:02:12.046
That's at pH seven only for a strong

00:02:12.127 --> 00:02:14.766
acid with a strong base, and it's never

00:02:14.927 --> 00:02:15.966
just the top.

00:02:16.287 --> 00:02:18.526
For both curves it's at twenty five point

00:02:18.687 --> 00:02:21.806
zero zero millilitres, as the moles said, because

00:02:21.847 --> 00:02:24.366
a titre counts moles, not strength.

00:02:25.007 --> 00:02:27.646
So for an unknown acid, that volume gives

00:02:27.727 --> 00:02:31.566
the concentration: here, zero point one zero zero

00:02:31.807 --> 00:02:32.926
moles per litre.

00:02:34.134 --> 00:02:36.133
Now read the pH at that volume.

00:02:36.614 --> 00:02:40.213
Hydrochloric acid gives seven, and ethanoic acid eight

00:02:40.374 --> 00:02:43.733
point seven two, because ethanoate makes the solution

00:02:43.814 --> 00:02:44.573
basic.

00:02:44.854 --> 00:02:47.253
And as in the indicator video, the jumps

00:02:47.334 --> 00:02:50.693
differ: the hydrochloric acid curve leaps from about

00:02:50.774 --> 00:02:53.653
four to ten, but the ethanoic acid curve

00:02:53.734 --> 00:02:56.453
only from about seven point four, because its

00:02:56.614 --> 00:02:58.613
buffer held the pH up until the last

00:02:58.694 --> 00:02:59.893
few drops.

00:03:00.014 --> 00:03:02.213
So a bigger jump doesn't mean a faster

00:03:02.294 --> 00:03:04.373
reaction, just less buffering.

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:07.840
What about a strong acid titrated with a

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:11.120
weak base, like hydrochloric acid with ammonia?

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:13.840
It starts at pH one, like the first

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:14.320
curve.

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:17.440
But the jump is lower and shorter, from

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:20.160
about four to six point six, around an

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:23.040
equivalence pH of five point two eight, because

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:25.280
ammonium makes the solution acidic.

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:28.640
And after equivalence it levels off near nine,

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:32.160
not above twelve: extra ammonia is weak, and

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:34.240
with ammonium it forms a buffer.

00:03:35.467 --> 00:03:38.426
A conductivity meter tells the same story another

00:03:38.507 --> 00:03:39.026
way.

00:03:39.307 --> 00:03:42.666
Hydronium and hydroxide conduct far better than other

00:03:42.747 --> 00:03:45.306
ions, because they can pass a proton from

00:03:45.387 --> 00:03:47.386
one water molecule to the next.

00:03:47.707 --> 00:03:51.306
As sodium hydroxide goes into hydrochloric acid, each

00:03:51.467 --> 00:03:54.586
fast hydronium is replaced by a slower sodium

00:03:54.667 --> 00:03:57.466
ion, so the conductivity falls.

00:03:57.627 --> 00:04:00.906
After equivalence, extra hydroxide piles up, and it

00:04:00.987 --> 00:04:02.026
rises.

00:04:02.107 --> 00:04:04.266
The lowest point marks equivalence.

00:04:05.467 --> 00:04:08.506
Ethanoic acid starts with few ions, so its

00:04:08.587 --> 00:04:10.186
conductivity starts low.

00:04:10.747 --> 00:04:13.706
As base is added, sodium and ethanoate ions

00:04:13.867 --> 00:04:16.026
build up, so it rises gently.

00:04:16.667 --> 00:04:19.626
Those ions are slower than hydronium, but each

00:04:19.707 --> 00:04:21.866
portion of base makes far more of them

00:04:21.947 --> 00:04:23.946
than the few hydronium ions there were at

00:04:23.987 --> 00:04:24.586
the start.

00:04:25.387 --> 00:04:29.066
After equivalence, fast hydroxide ions add on, and

00:04:29.107 --> 00:04:30.666
it rises more steeply.

00:04:30.987 --> 00:04:33.466
Here, equivalence is the bend where the slope

00:04:33.547 --> 00:04:35.226
changes, not a minimum.

00:04:36.434 --> 00:04:37.873
So here's what to remember.

00:04:38.274 --> 00:04:41.913
At half-equivalence, pH equals p K a.

00:04:42.194 --> 00:04:44.513
Find equivalence in the middle of the jump,

00:04:44.594 --> 00:04:47.193
halve the volume, and read the pH there.

00:04:47.474 --> 00:04:49.713
It works because the two partners are equal

00:04:49.754 --> 00:04:50.433
at that point.

00:04:50.914 --> 00:04:54.433
And for conductivity: hydronium and hydroxide are the

00:04:54.514 --> 00:04:55.713
best conductors.

00:04:56.934 --> 00:04:57.653
Your turn.

00:04:58.134 --> 00:05:01.493
A student titrates twenty point zero zero millilitres

00:05:01.574 --> 00:05:04.533
of an acid, H A, with zero point

00:05:04.614 --> 00:05:07.573
one zero zero zero moles per litre sodium

00:05:07.774 --> 00:05:10.173
hydroxide, and plots this curve.

00:05:10.534 --> 00:05:13.173
Using two features of the curve, decide whether

00:05:13.254 --> 00:05:15.653
H A is strong or weak.

00:05:15.814 --> 00:05:19.653
Then calculate its concentration, and its K a.

00:05:19.814 --> 00:05:21.013
Pause here to answer.

00:05:31.434 --> 00:05:34.073
It's weak: there's a buffer region before the

00:05:34.154 --> 00:05:36.793
jump, and the pH at equivalence is above

00:05:36.874 --> 00:05:37.353
seven.

00:05:37.914 --> 00:05:39.993
The jump's middle is at twenty point zero

00:05:40.154 --> 00:05:43.593
millilitres, so the concentration is zero point one

00:05:43.674 --> 00:05:45.873
zero zero moles per litre.

00:05:46.234 --> 00:05:48.713
Half of twenty is ten, where the pH

00:05:48.794 --> 00:05:51.353
is four point five seven, so K a

00:05:51.514 --> 00:05:53.273
is two point seven times ten to the

00:05:53.354 --> 00:05:54.473
minus five.

00:05:54.794 --> 00:05:56.953
Reading p K a at the equivalence point

00:05:57.194 --> 00:05:58.073
is the slip.

00:05:59.300 --> 00:06:00.260
Last one.

00:06:00.580 --> 00:06:03.540
Zero point one zero zero moles per litre

00:06:03.620 --> 00:06:06.820
sodium hydroxide is added to twenty point zero

00:06:06.980 --> 00:06:10.020
zero millilitres of hydrochloric acid.

00:06:10.100 --> 00:06:12.740
The conductivity falls to a minimum at sixteen

00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:15.780
point zero millilitres, then rises.

00:06:16.100 --> 00:06:18.580
Explain the shape of the graph, and calculate

00:06:18.660 --> 00:06:21.460
the concentration of the hydrochloric acid.

00:06:21.620 --> 00:06:22.820
Pause here to answer.

00:06:33.234 --> 00:06:36.433
Before equivalence, each hydronium is replaced by a

00:06:36.594 --> 00:06:39.513
sodium ion, which conducts less well, so the

00:06:39.554 --> 00:06:40.913
conductivity falls.

00:06:41.474 --> 00:06:44.433
After it, extra hydroxide makes it rise.

00:06:44.994 --> 00:06:47.953
The minimum is equivalence, so the concentration is

00:06:48.034 --> 00:06:50.753
zero point zero eight zero zero moles per

00:06:50.834 --> 00:06:51.233
litre.

00:06:52.194 --> 00:06:54.353
Saying it falls because the ions are used

00:06:54.434 --> 00:06:56.913
up misses that the number of ions stays

00:06:57.034 --> 00:06:57.873
about the same.

00:06:59.100 --> 00:07:02.860
For any titration curve: read the starting pH,

00:07:02.940 --> 00:07:05.500
look for a buffer region, find equivalence in

00:07:05.580 --> 00:07:07.820
the middle of the jump, read its volume

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:10.220
and pH, and halve the volume for p

00:07:10.300 --> 00:07:10.780
K a.

00:07:11.340 --> 00:07:13.860
For conductivity, look for the minimum or the

00:07:13.900 --> 00:07:14.380
bend.

00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:18.620
Next: when a direct titration won't work, back

00:07:18.780 --> 00:07:21.180
titrations and conductivity titrations.

00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:23.840
Here are the key notes.

00:07:24.080 --> 00:07:26.000
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:26.080 --> 00:07:26.880
to copy them.
